    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d33-a1">

                <pb facs="0102" n="42" sameAs="frus:frus1895p1/pg42"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 128.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. de Burlet to Mr. Ewing</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ministry of Foreign Affairs</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-06-13"><hi rendition="#italic">June 13,
                        1895</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Minister</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge
                    the receipt of your excellency’s note, dated the 6th of this month, by which you
                    are so kind as to inform me of the desire expressed by the Government of
                    Venezuela to see the United States employ its good offices in reestablishing
                    relations between Venezuela and Belgium.</p>
                <p>The representative of France having been treated in the same manner as the agent
                    of Belgium, the Government of the King wishes in the first place to have an
                    understanding with that of the French Republic. Moreover, it is awaiting the
                    speedy arrival of M. Ledeganck, its consul-general chargé d’affaires at Caracas,
                    who, for family reasons, was not able to leave that city as soon as the Marquis
                    Ripert Monchar, envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary of France.</p>
                <p>Be so kind as to accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. de Burlet</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
